{{also|électrolyte}}
==English==
{{wp}}

===Etymology===
From {{affix|en|electro-|-lyte}}, from {{m|en|electrolysis}}. {{coin|en|Q8750|in=1834}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ɪˈlɛk.tɹɵˌlaɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vininn126-electrolyte.wav|Audio (US)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|chemistry}} A [[substance]] that, in [[solution]] or when [[molten]], [[ionize]]s and [[conduct]]s [[electricity]].
# {{lb|en|chemistry}} A [[solution]] containing such substances, which therefore also conducts electricity. 
#: {{ux|en|Some modern lead-acid batteries are sealed because they never need to have any '''electrolyte''' added during their service life.}}
# {{lb|en|chemistry|often}} Any of the various [[ion]]s in such a [[solution]], or ({{l|en|metonym|metonymically}}) their {{l|en|concentration}} therein. 
## {{lb|en|physiology|medicine}} Any of the various {{l|en|biometal}} [[ion]]s (such as [[sodium]] or [[chloride]]) that [[regulate]] the [[electric charge]] on [[cell]]s and the [[flow]] of [[water]] across their [[membrane]]s, or ({{l|en|metonym|metonymically}}) such an ion's {{l|en|concentration}} in a {{l|en|bodily fluid}}, usually and especially {{l|en|blood#Noun}}, {{l|en|blood serum#Noun}}, or {{l|en|urine}}. 
##: {{ux|en|Lab tests to consider under these circumstances include serum '''electrolytes''', serum glucose, and urine protein.}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|en|dyselectrolytaemia}}
* {{l|en|electrolytic}}

